{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1791184814,"nonce":"kHFHKXjb8i1FMkyQcEO6la/lpGc=","target":"a556a5c75fa1297fd9fea37719efa0c0f9d07b1ffebc2cf6110be518b3e415d3","message":"**What is wrong.** A Remedy can be approved or declined from Slack (ac9b492, 9be848b), but only by a Slack member linked to an oto user who holds the approval grant. Nothing can create that link. `Service.LinkSlackIdentity` (`internal/identity/service/reads.go:329`) has no caller: there is no route, no CLI command and no UI. The unlinked reply tells a member to \"ask whoever runs oto to link\", and nobody can.\n\n**The demonstration.** `grep -rn LinkSlackIdentity internal cmd` finds only its definition and tests. Every Slack approve click is refused, including one from a grant holder.\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0054 §2 makes Slack one of the two places approval happens. Until a link exists, that half of the ticket is dead code. The link is also security-relevant: it decides **whose approval a Slack click counts as**, so a wrong link lets one person's clicks count as another person's approval and defeats double approval (ADR 0054 §4).\n\n**Where.** The owner ruled on 2026-10-05 that linking is **self-service in the UI**, not a host-shell command. The design:\n- The flow starts in Slack, because only Slack can prove a Slack identity, and oto already verifies the signature on every interaction payload (`internal/channels/api/slack_interactions.go`). When an unlinked member clicks, oto answers with an ephemeral, seen only by that member, carrying a one-time **link code**. The code is bound to the verified `(org, team_id, user_id)`. It is random (≥ 40 bits), stored only as a hash, single-use, and expires after 10 minutes.\n- The member signs in to oto and enters the code on their own profile page. oto shows a confirmation naming the Slack member and workspace — *\"clicks from this Slack account will count as you\"* — before linking through `LinkSlackIdentity`. That call already re-points a shadow member and retires it.\n- A Slack identity already linked to another real user is refused, never silently moved. A user can unlink themselves from the same page.\n- Wrong codes are rate-limited per user and per code. Every link and unlink is recorded with its actor.\n- The docs say plainly that a link code is a credential: anyone who enters your code in their own oto session makes your Slack clicks count as theirs. The confirmation screen and the recorded fact are the mitigation.\n- No HTTP route links an arbitrary user. The route only ever links the **signed-in** user.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** An unlinked click gets a code; entering it while signed in links that Slack member to the signed-in user, after confirmation; a used, expired or wrong code is refused, and repeated wrong codes are rate-limited; a Slack identity linked to another real user is refused; unlink works; a linked grant holder's Slack approve then counts through the same service path as the UI; tests cover each; the docs carry the credential warning.\n\n**Blocked by.** `ac9b492`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — the owner has ruled (self-service in the UI). Source: ac9b492 implementation, 2026-10-05.","files":null}]}